WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.942
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:09.524
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.824 --> 00:00:15.739
Unit three: Social Movements. Today you will report what activists say and do with reporting verbs.

4
00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:18.610
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:23.067
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:24.860
Activism.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.986
Activism means action taken to bring about political or social change.

8
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:33.187
Student activism helped to change the law.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.331
Your turn. Say: Activism.

10
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.168
Activism.

11
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:41.976
Protest.

12
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:46.388
Protest means a public event where people show they disagree.

13
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:49.679
The protest remained peaceful throughout the day.

14
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:53.156
Your turn. Say: Protest.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:56.908
Protest.

16
00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.907
Advocacy group.

17
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:04.041
Advocacy group means an organisation that supports a particular cause.

18
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.393
The advocacy group published a report on housing.

19
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:11.065
Your turn. Say: Advocacy group.

20
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.180
Advocacy group.

21
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:17.080
Grassroots.

22
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:22.312
Grassroots means started by ordinary people rather than leaders.

23
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:25.706
A grassroots campaign spread across the country.

24
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:29.097
Your turn. Say: Grassroots.

25
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:33.225
Grassroots.

26
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:34.915
Petition.

27
00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:39.100
Petition means a formal request signed by many people.

28
00:01:39.717 --> 00:01:42.390
The petition gathered fifty thousand signatures.

29
00:01:42.983 --> 00:01:45.427
Your turn. Say: Petition.

30
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.186
Petition.

31
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.241
Civil rights.

32
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.747
Civil rights means the rights of citizens to equality and freedom.

33
00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.744
The movement fought for equal civil rights.

34
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.304
Your turn. Say: Civil rights.

35
00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.493
Civil rights.

36
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.298
Solidarity.

37
00:02:09.283 --> 00:02:12.920
Solidarity means unity between people who share a goal.

38
00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:16.422
Workers showed solidarity by marching together.

39
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:19.797
Your turn. Say: Solidarity.

40
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:23.843
Solidarity.

41
00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:25.631
Awareness.

42
00:02:26.617 --> 00:02:30.346
Awareness means knowledge that something exists or is important.

43
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.170
The march raised awareness of the problem.

44
00:02:33.783 --> 00:02:36.482
Your turn. Say: Awareness.

45
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:40.462
Awareness.

46
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:45.103
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:53.061
Visal, a headline says 'Activists urged the government to listen.' How do reporting verbs work with a person and an action?

48
00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:07.727
Some take object + to-infinitive: urged, warned, encouraged. Others take a preposition + gerund: accused them of ignoring, blamed him for delaying, apologised for being late.

49
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:10.642
And some take a that-clause?

50
00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:24.433
Yes: insisted that, announced that. But 'suggest' never takes object + to: we say 'suggested starting a petition', not 'suggested us to start'.

51
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:26.865
Why are they useful?

52
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:36.314
They let a writer report a protest accurately without quoting. Compare 'they said please' with 'they appealed to the council to act'.

53
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.153
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.017 --> 00:03:44.872
Grammar focus: Reporting verbs: urge plus to and accuse plus of -ing.

55
00:03:45.172 --> 00:03:46.452
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:50.612
The advocacy group urged the council to protect civil rights.

57
00:03:51.517 --> 00:03:54.329
Protesters accused the minister of ignoring the petition.

58
00:03:55.217 --> 00:03:58.540
Organisers suggested starting a grassroots campaign.

59
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:07.397
Use urge / warn / encourage + object + to-verb; accuse / blame + object + of / for + -ing; suggest + -ing (never suggest someone to do).

60
00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.801
Be careful! Don't say: She suggested me to join.

61
00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:16.010
Say: She suggested joining.

62
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.704
'Suggest' does not take object + to.

63
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.083
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:24.425
Voices from the Street.

65
00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:29.279
Social change often begins with ordinary people who refuse to stay silent.

66
00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:35.947
Grassroots activism grows when a small group organises a petition and encourages neighbours to sign it.

67
00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:43.647
Protesters may accuse leaders of ignoring public needs, and advocacy groups often urge governments to protect civil rights.

68
00:04:44.633 --> 00:04:49.884
Marches build awareness, but solidarity between different communities makes a movement last.

69
00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:55.198
History shows that peaceful pressure, repeated for years, can change the law.

70
00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.453
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.317 --> 00:05:03.595
The group urged the mayor blank the park.

72
00:05:03.895 --> 00:05:07.397
A, to protect. B, protecting. C, protect.

73
00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:12.933
The answer is A: to protect.

74
00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.269
They accused the minister blank the petition.

75
00:05:16.569 --> 00:05:20.431
A, to ignore. B, of ignoring. C, for ignore.

76
00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:26.187
The answer is B: of ignoring.

77
00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.557
A formal request signed by many people is a blank.

78
00:05:30.857 --> 00:05:34.476
A, petition. B, protest. C, treaty.

79
00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:40.103
The answer is A: petition.

80
00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.041
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.341 --> 00:05:49.838
A, She suggested me to join.. B, She suggested me joining.. C, She suggested joining the march..

82
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.537
The answer is C: She suggested joining the march..

83
00:05:57.683 --> 00:06:00.658
According to the reading, what makes a movement last?

84
00:06:00.958 --> 00:06:05.180
A, silence. B, solidarity. C, a single march.

85
00:06:08.733 --> 00:06:10.698
The answer is B: solidarity.

86
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.353
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.217 --> 00:06:19.679
What makes a social movement successful in creating change?

88
00:06:28.483 --> 00:06:29.926
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.226 --> 00:06:59.746
A social movement succeeds when it combines a clear, achievable goal with broad support. Grassroots organisers must keep people motivated over many years, build solidarity across different communities and gain media attention. Successful movements also use peaceful pressure, such as petitions and protests, while offering realistic solutions that policy makers can turn into law.

90
00:07:00.046 --> 00:07:01.697
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:02.583 --> 00:07:08.951
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reporting verbs: urge plus to and accuse plus of -ing.

92
00:07:09.251 --> 00:07:13.970
Today's value is solidarity. Stand together with others who face injustice.

93
00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.655
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:16.955 --> 00:07:19.782
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

